**Original Paper**

# A Machine Learning Depression Screener Developed in the Korea Community Health Survey and Externally Validated in the US National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey Across the COVID-19 Period: Development and External Validation Study

Authors: [to be completed]

Corresponding author: [to be completed]

---

## Structured Abstract

**Background.** Depression is a leading cause of disability, and scalable, low-cost screening is needed. Most machine learning (ML) depression models from national surveys report internal validation only; whether they transport to another country and remain stable across the COVID-19 shift is rarely tested.

**Objective.** To develop an ML model for current depression (Patient Health Questionnaire-9 [PHQ-9] ￣10) in the Korea Community Health Survey (KCHS) and externally validate it in the US National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES) across the pre- and post-COVID-19 periods, as the engine for a deployable digital triage tool.

**Methods.** Penalized logistic regression, random forest, and gradient boosting were developed in KCHS (2017?2025; pooled n=2,048,510; outcome-stratified 500,000-record training set and 100,000-record hold-out) using a harmonized 17-predictor set shared with NHANES?14 protocol-specified predictors plus employment, oral health, and food insecurity added by a documented amendment locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted, though after the 14-predictor external result was known. All algorithm, feature-representation and hyperparameter selection used KCHS-internal cross-validation only; the trained model was applied to NHANES without refitting. Evaluation was complex-survey?weighted with rescaled cluster-bootstrap CIs, SHAP interpretation and subgroup fairness. Temporal validation contrasted the NCHS-defined pre-pandemic (2017?March 2020; n=7,888) and post-pandemic (2021?2023; n=5,210) cycles.

**Results.** Gradient boosting (LightGBM) was the primary model. Internal AUROC was 0.832 (95% CI 0.823?0.841), meeting the ￣0.80 target (H1). External discrimination remained clinically useful but lower (pre 0.772, 95% CI 0.735?0.808; post 0.769, 0.739?0.799); calibration deteriorated (slope 0.66?0.68; calibration-in-the-large +0.49 and +1.11, ie, under-prediction) and was fully restored by intercept/slope recalibration (slope⊥0.99, CITL⊥0.00; H2, H3 supported). Against an independently tuned 14-predictor model, the three added predictors improved external discrimination (pre 0.749⊥0.772, 汁+0.023, 95% CI +0.013 to +0.033; post 0.753⊥0.769, 汁+0.016, +0.008 to +0.023; both *P*<.001). Applying the analysis plan's parsimony margin, with predictor order and item count fixed inside the development data, selected a 5-item model (0.809 internal, 0.742 pre, 0.746 post; H4 partially supported); a 7-item variant transports better (0.819/0.760/0.758) but was chosen post hoc. Leakage-controlled optimization (>450 experiments) did not exceed ~0.77 externally, and an oracle fitted directly to NHANES reached only 0.765?0.772, whereas the same predictors reached 0.81?0.82 in equally sized Korean samples.

**Conclusions.** A Korea-developed depression screener transported to the US and across the pandemic with clinically useful discrimination and fully recoverable calibration. The cross-national ceiling (~0.77) persisted against sample size, algorithm choice, covariate-shift correction and an in-domain oracle, and is therefore a property of the transportable predictor set rather than a modeling failure. A low-burden 5- to 7-item version, paired with local recalibration, is a plausible scalable digital triage aid.

**Keywords:** depression; mass screening; machine learning; supervised machine learning; models, statistical; external validation; transportability; concept shift; COVID-19; mobile health; Patient Health Questionnaire; Republic of Korea.

---

## 1. Introduction

Depression is among the leading causes of years lived with disability worldwide [1], and its detection at the community and primary-care level remains incomplete. Brief self-report instruments such as the PHQ-9 enable population-scale screening [3], yet applying a fixed cut-off uniformly across heterogeneous populations does not prioritize those at highest risk. Risk-stratification tools that combine routinely available sociodemographic and behavioral information could support more efficient triage and, when delivered through web or mobile interfaces, could scale at low marginal cost?an application squarely within the scope of internet-based health services.

ML models for depression have been developed repeatedly from large national surveys, including the KCHS and the Korea National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey [11,12], typically reporting strong internal discrimination. However, most published models are assessed only through internal validation. Internal performance is an optimistic indicator of real-world behavior: it does not establish that a model transports to a different sampling frame, health system, or population, and it does not test stability under temporal distribution shift.

The COVID-19 pandemic constitutes a natural distribution shift for mental-health prediction [2,10]. Population distributions of depressive symptoms and of their behavioral correlates changed during the pandemic, and a model trained before 2020 may show degraded calibration afterward even if discrimination is preserved. Whether recalibration?updating only the intercept and slope without re-estimating predictor effects [6,7]?restores clinical usefulness is a practically important, under-studied question for deployed screening tools.

This study addresses these gaps with a development-and-external-validation design well suited to the outcome. Depression is operationalized identically in both surveys (nine-item PHQ-9, 0?3 scoring, sum ￣10), minimizing outcome-harmonization error, while the surveys differ in country, health system, racial/ethnic composition, and survey mode, making NHANES a substantively external validation set. NHANES additionally provides NCHS-defined pre- and post-pandemic data, enabling temporal validation within the same external population.

We pursued four aims: (1) develop an ML depression-screening model in KCHS; (2) externally validate it in US NHANES (geographic transportability); (3) validate it separately pre/post-COVID-19 and quantify recalibration (temporal transportability); and (4) derive a parsimonious model for a low-burden digital tool. We hypothesized: **H1** the full model achieves internal AUROC ￣0.80; **H2** external discrimination remains clinically useful while calibration may deteriorate; **H3** recalibration substantially improves calibration; **H4** the parsimonious model incurs only a small performance loss.

---

## 2. Methods

### 2.1 Study design and reporting standards
Retrospective secondary-data study developing a clinical prediction model with external validation; reported per TRIPOD+AI (2024) [4], risk of bias self-assessed with PROBAST [5]. The study protocol and statistical analysis plan?including the four hypotheses?were written and dated before any analysis. The predictor-set expansion described in ＝2.5 is a documented amendment written *after* the 14-predictor model had already been developed and applied to NHANES, and locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted (＝2.5.1). Multimedia Appendix 2 provides the year-specific harmonization crosswalk together with an English rendition of the locked amendment; the unmodified original protocol, statistical analysis plan, and amendment document will be deposited with the analysis code, hyperparameter grids, and model card in a public repository (DOI [to be inserted]; see Data Availability).

### 2.2 Data sources
**Development ? KCHS.** Nationwide KDCA survey of community-dwelling adults, annual in-person household interviews (~230,000/year), stratified/clustered/weighted complex-sample design. Pooled development sample: KCHS 2017?2025 with PHQ-9 administered; n=2,048,510. From this pooled sample, an outcome-stratified random split created a **500,000-record training set** and a **100,000-record internal hold-out set** used for all internal performance estimates; the remaining 1,448,510 records were not used. The training-set size was fixed for computational tractability before any external evaluation, and a subsequent check confirmed that expanding it toward the full pooled sample did not improve?and slightly reduced?external discrimination (＝3.5).
**External validation ? US NHANES.** NCHS survey combining household interview and mobile examination center assessment; depression via the DPQ (PHQ-9) module. Two periods used as separate validation sets: pre-pandemic (2017?March 2020, "P_" files; n=7,888) and post-pandemic (August 2021?August 2023, "_L" files; n=5,210).

### 2.3 Participants
Adults ￣20 years with all nine PHQ-9 items answered. Pregnant respondents were retained in the main analysis. A prior physician diagnosis of depression is not recorded in the KCHS 2017?2025 questionnaires and therefore could not be used for exclusion or sensitivity analysis; this is noted as a limitation.

### 2.4 Outcome
Current depression = PHQ-9 total ￣10, computed identically in both surveys (nine items, 0?3). KCHS items recoded 1?4⊥0?3 to match NHANES. Sensitivity: ￣5 cut-off.

### 2.5 Candidate predictors and harmonization
Only variables definable identically in both surveys entered the harmonized model. The final harmonized set comprised **17 predictors** (Table 1): the 14 protocol-specified predictors?age, sex, education, household income (within-survey quintiles), self-rated health, partnership, living alone, smoking, alcohol, physical activity, sleep duration, BMI, hypertension, diabetes?plus three added predictors: **employment** (KCHS soa_01z1 ㏒ NHANES OCD150), **self-rated oral health** (ora_01z1 ㏒ OHQ845), and **household food insecurity** (nue_01z1 ㏒ FSDAD). Concept-level harmonization used a label-based per-year crosswalk on the KCHS side and cycle-specific code branching on the NHANES side; self-rated health was harmonized to NHANES HUQ010 (correcting a Table 1 file-code error for HSD010/HSQ).

**2.5.1 Predictor-set amendment and selection provenance.** The three added predictors were identified by crosswalking all 616 KCHS concepts against the NHANES codebook for both cycles (a 175-variable extract of candidate items at the time of the amendment; a later, exhaustive extraction identified 265 variables common to both cycles). The amendment was locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted, but not before the study as a whole: the 14-predictor model had already been developed and transferred, and the locked document explicitly records the then-known internal AUROC (0.824) and the then-observed external level (~0.75) when stating its expectations (Multimedia Appendix 2). The expansion decision therefore could not be tuned on 17-predictor external results, but it was not blind to how the 14-predictor model had performed externally, and we report it as a documented amendment rather than as a blinded pre-registration. The amendment required a candidate to be (i) available in both NHANES cycles, (ii) collected in more than one KCHS survey year, and (iii) not circular with the PHQ-9 outcome. Employment, self-rated oral health, and household food insecurity were the three concepts adopted. Candidates rejected at this stage failed on single-year collection (e.g., dyslipidemia, osteoporosis, arthritis), concept mismatch (KCHS records only *awareness of early warning signs* for stroke and myocardial infarction, not physician diagnosis), or absence from the NHANES pre-pandemic cycle (functional limitation). Criteria (i)?(iii) themselves use survey documentation and variable availability only, and no NHANES outcome association was estimated for any candidate before the amendment was locked. Each added predictor has an established association with depression in the epidemiological literature?food insecurity (pooled odds ratio ?1.4) [13], unemployment (?1.9?2.1) [14], and poor oral health (a bidirectional relationship) [15]?supporting biological and social plausibility rather than data-driven selection. Circular/outcome-contaminated variables (PHQ items, suicidal ideation, EQ-5D anxiety/depression, happiness, perceived stress, mental-health service use including NHANES HUQ090) were pre-specified for exclusion.

**Post-hoc candidate screening (reported for completeness).** The later exhaustive extraction surfaced further harmonizable items, some of which would also have satisfied criteria (i)?(iii)?notably public-assistance receipt, alcohol frequency, alcohol quantity, binge drinking, and weekend sleep duration; the alcohol and sleep items are refinements of predictors already in the set. After the primary analysis was complete we screened all of them, comparing the direction of their adjusted odds ratios with depression across KCHS, NHANES-pre, and NHANES-post, and then testing them in the model. Alcohol quantity, binge drinking, and weekend sleep reversed direction between countries; public-assistance receipt was directionally consistent but added no external discrimination when included (汁AUROC <0.001; ＝3.5). No item was added, so the predictor set is unchanged from the locked amendment. Because this screening inspected NHANES outcome associations, it is reported as a post-hoc diagnostic and was not used for selection. Two robustness analyses further bound the amendment's contribution: the 14-predictor protocol-specified model is reported alongside the 17-predictor model throughout, and a food-insecurity leave-out (16-predictor) shows the incremental gain does not rest on the most weakly harmonized predictor (＝3.4). Model fitting, hyperparameter tuning, and primary-model selection used KCHS-internal cross-validation only, with no NHANES data.

**Harmonization cautions.** Alcohol, physical activity, and income use different instruments and were reduced to conservatively comparable definitions. Only the alcohol definition could be varied in sensitivity analysis (＝3.7); the harmonized data carry a single binary physical-activity indicator and a within-survey income quintile, so no alternative definition of those two was available. The protocol specified alcohol as current drinking plus at-risk drinking, whereas the harmonized variable uses current drinking alone because the NHANES quantity item was not carried through harmonization; this is recorded as a definition-level deviation (Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S11). BMI is self-reported in KCHS, measured in NHANES. Living-alone is unavailable in the NHANES pre-pandemic DEMO file (100% missing) and is retained as missing (not imputed to a constant) to avoid fabricating a constant. Several KCHS items are also structurally missing for whole survey years because the module rotates: food insecurity was not fielded in 2020?2021 (22.1% missing overall), sleep duration was not fielded in 2023 (11.3%), and BMI could be neither read nor derived in 2019 (13.1%). These gaps are year-structural rather than respondent-level. They are handled differently by predictor type (＝2.6): food insecurity is binary and its missingness is preserved for the tree learner, whereas sleep duration and BMI are continuous and are median-imputed, so the 2023 sleep block and the 2019 BMI block are filled with a training-set constant. The imputation sensitivity analyses (＝2.12) bound the effect of that choice, and the food-insecurity leave-out bounds the effect of the largest structural gap. Food insecurity additionally uses a single KCHS item versus the 10-item NHANES module. The full year-by-year variable map is provided as Multimedia Appendix 2.

### 2.6 Missing data
All missing-data handling was performed by a preprocessing pipeline fitted in the development training set and applied unchanged to the internal hold-out and to each external set (no leakage). In the gradient-boosting primary model, binary predictors were passed through with missingness preserved and handled natively by the tree learner?so structurally missing binary items such as living-alone in NHANES pre are never imputed to a constant?while continuous predictors were median-imputed and categorical predictors mode-imputed within the same pipeline. For the linear comparator, continuous predictors were median-imputed, categorical predictors mode-imputed, and binary predictors mode-imputed with an added missing indicator. Household income (within-survey quintile), self-rated health and self-rated oral health are ordinal scores and were modeled as numeric variables rather than as unordered categories, so they were median-imputed and standardized. Alternative strategies (missing indicators added for continuous and binary predictors; single chained-equations imputation of the continuous predictors) were examined in sensitivity analysis (＝2.12). A silent-column-omission check was added after a data-integrity audit (see ＝2.13).

### 2.7 Sample size
Development is not power-limiting given KCHS size: the 500,000-record training set contains approximately 18,000 events (about 1,000 events per candidate predictor) and the 100,000-record hold-out approximately 3,600 events. NHANES event counts (728 pre, 661 post; weighted prevalence 8.4% and 12.2%) govern external calibration and decision-curve precision.

### 2.8 Model development
Algorithms: penalized logistic regression (elastic net), random forest, and gradient boosting (LightGBM). Development proceeded in two selection steps, both confined to the KCHS training set. First, the gradient-boosting hyperparameters and the feature representation were chosen by a randomized search over 24 LightGBM configurations crossed with two representations?main effects only, versus main effects plus four candidate interaction terms, specified when the search was written but not named in any earlier document (age▼self-rated health, self-rated health▼oral health, age▼employment, income▼employment)?evaluated by 4-fold survey-weighted cross-validation in a 300,000-record subsample of the training set; the main-effects-only representation won, and adding interaction terms did not improve cross-validated discrimination. Second, all three algorithms were tuned over comparable 5-configuration grids under an identical protocol (5-fold stratified cross-validation in a 200,000-record subsample of the training set, scored by unweighted cross-validated AUROC) and compared at each algorithm's best configuration, so that no algorithm was advantaged by differential tuning effort. The analysis plan fixed the primary-model rule in advance: highest cross-validated AUROC, with differences smaller than 0.005 treated as ties and broken by proximity of the calibration slope to 1, and only then by parsimony. The pipeline as executed ranked the algorithms directly on AUROC and then calibration slope without constructing the tie band, so the plan's rule was checked retrospectively. **No NHANES data were used in any selection or tuning step.** Complex-survey weights were handled by unweighted training with weighted evaluation (probed in sensitivity). LightGBM (0.840) and random forest (0.835) differ by 0.0046 and therefore fall inside the tie band, so the plan's tie-break applies; it selects LightGBM, whose cross-validated calibration slope was 1.003 against 1.203 for random forest. Elastic net (0.822) lies outside the band and the parsimony criterion is not reached, so the retrospective check reproduces the model the pipeline selected (Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S11). LightGBM was refitted on the full 500,000-record training set; the selected hyperparameters and the full search grids are reported in the model card, which is deposited with the code (see Data Availability). The selected row-subsampling fraction is inactive in the deployed configuration because the subsampling frequency was left at its default of zero, so the fitted model uses all rows at every boosting iteration.

### 2.9 Performance evaluation
Discrimination (AUROC, AUPRC), calibration, and operating characteristics, all complex-survey?weighted. Calibration was summarized by the calibration slope and by calibration-in-the-large (CITL), the latter estimated as the intercept of a weighted logistic model with the linear predictor entered as an offset (ie, with the slope fixed at 1), together with the Brier score and expected calibration error (ECE); a positive CITL indicates that observed risk exceeds predicted risk. Ninety-five percent CIs are reported for AUROC and AUPRC only; calibration measures and operating characteristics are point estimates. CIs were obtained by a rescaled stratified cluster bootstrap with 1000 replicates, for cohort-level and subgroup estimates alike: within each design stratum, *n*<sub>h</sub>?1 primary sampling units were resampled with replacement and the survey weights rescaled by *n*<sub>h</sub>/(*n*<sub>h</sub>?1) times the selection count [16]. Rescaling is necessary because most NHANES strata contain only two primary sampling units, for which an ordinary within-stratum cluster bootstrap understates variance. We verified the implementation against a closed-form design-based (linearized ultimate-cluster) standard error for weighted depression prevalence: bootstrap 0.00466 vs design 0.00471 in the pre-pandemic cycle and 0.00802 vs 0.00796 in the post-pandemic cycle, i.e. agreement within 1%. For KCHS the stratum is defined as survey year crossed with the design stratum, because weights are normalized within year and stratum codes recur across years. Subgroup intervals are domain estimates: replicate weights are generated once on the full cohort design and then applied to each subgroup, rather than rebuilding the design after subsetting. Strata left with a single primary sampling unit are pooled into one pseudo-stratum so their variance contribution is not lost. Clinical utility by decision-curve analysis [8].

### 2.10 External validation strategy
Geographic: KCHS-trained model applied to NHANES without refitting. Temporal/COVID: evaluated separately in pre and post cycles. Recalibration: intercept/slope recalibration applied out-of-sample (cross-fit) and recovery quantified?updating calibration only, without re-estimating predictor effects [6,7].

### 2.11 Interpretability, fairness, deployment
SHAP global importance [9] and cross-survey rank concordance of predictor importance (Spearman correlation between KCHS and each NHANES cycle) as a mechanistic view of transportability; fairness by weighted subgroup AUROC and calibration slope across sex, age, education, and (NHANES) race/ethnicity; and a parsimonious model for a low-burden self-administered form. Both the predictor order and the item count were determined entirely inside the development training set, which was split into a 450,000-record fitting set and a 50,000-record selection set: predictors were entered in descending order of global SHAP importance computed on a random 20,000-record subsample of the fitting set, refitted at every item count from 1 to 17, and the item count was chosen on selection-set AUROC. The item count follows the analysis plan's margin ? the smallest set within 0.02 AUROC of the full model, subject to a 5?10 item ceiling; a variant using a stricter 0.01 margin is reported alongside it and flagged as post hoc (＝3.6). The internal hold-out and both NHANES cohorts were used only for evaluation (＝3.6). Positioned as a screening/triage aid, not a diagnostic.

### 2.12 Sensitivity analyses
Pre-specified and additional analyses (exact values in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7): outcome cut-off (￣5 as well as ￣10, evaluated internally and in both external cohorts), removal of the three added predictors (⊥14), removal of food insecurity only (⊥16), weighting approach (weighted vs unweighted training), alternative imputation (missing indicators for continuous and binary predictors; single chained-equations imputation of continuous predictors), an age lower bound of 30 years applied to the training *and* evaluation samples, and?as pre-specified in the protocol?a continuous-score analysis evaluating how the model ranks the untransformed PHQ-9 total, and alternative definitions of the alcohol predictor (drinking at least twice weekly, binge drinking, or all three indicators together in place of current drinking). The self-report (KCHS) versus measured (NHANES) difference in BMI is inherent to the data sources and is addressed under harmonization cautions (＝2.5) and Limitations rather than by swapping measurements. All analyses were performed in Python 3.14 (scikit-learn 1.9.0, lightgbm 4.6.0, shap 0.52.0, numpy 2.4.5, pandas 3.0.3, scipy 1.18.0, pyreadstat 1.3.5, matplotlib 3.10.9); complex-survey weighting, the stratified cluster bootstrap, calibration measures, and decision-curve net benefit were implemented directly rather than through a survey-analysis package, and the implementations are released with the code.

### 2.13 Data-integrity audit
An adversarial audit corrected three silent data-processing defects before final analysis: (1) living-alone fabricated to a constant in NHANES pre (DMDHHSIZ absent ⊥ constant imputation) ? corrected to preserve missingness; (2) NHANES post physical-activity coded 0 for all respondents due to string-typed unit fields mis-parsed to NaN ? corrected with explicit unit mapping; (3) an income refusal code (77777) not treated as missing, placing non-respondents in the top income quintile ? corrected. These affected data integrity but not the substantive conclusions.

### 2.14 Ethical Considerations
KCHS and NHANES are public, de-identified secondary datasets. NHANES data collection was approved by the NCHS Research Ethics Review Board, and KCHS is conducted under the authority of the KDCA. The present secondary analysis was reviewed by the Institutional Review Board of Daejeon University, which granted an exemption from review (approval number [to be inserted]) on the grounds that the study uses publicly available and de-identified data, collects and records no personal identifiers, and poses no more than minimal risk (no participant contact, intervention, or biological sampling). No re-identification was attempted, no compensation was involved, and no individually identifiable information is reported.

---

## 3. Results

### 3.1 Participant characteristics
Weighted depression prevalence (PHQ-9 ￣10): KCHS 3.6%, NHANES pre 8.4%, post 12.2% (Figure 1). The surveys differed markedly on measured covariates?e.g., mean BMI 23.7 (KCHS) vs 29.8 (NHANES), and weighted food-insecurity prevalence 3.7% in KCHS vs 16.4% and 17.2% in the NHANES cycles, computed among respondents with the item recorded (missing 22.1%, 6.0% and 6.3%, respectively; every characteristic in Table S6 uses available-case denominators)?consistent with genuinely distinct populations and, for food insecurity, with the single-item-vs-module measurement difference. DPQ item non-response was substantial in both NHANES cycles and much larger after the pandemic: among adults ￣20 years, 14.3% of the pre-pandemic and 32.7% of the post-pandemic respondents left three or more PHQ-9 items unanswered, yielding the complete-case analytic samples of 7,888 and 5,210 (item-level detail in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S10). Non-response was differential?higher among respondents with less than high-school education (20.6% pre, 47.3% post) and among Asian and Black respondents?so the external cohorts, and particularly the post-pandemic fairness estimates, should be read as complete-case results. Reassuringly, adding respondents missing only one or two items using their person-mean changed the observed prevalence by ¯0.1 percentage points in both cycles (unweighted: 9.23%⊥9.27% pre, 12.69%⊥12.78% post). Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S6.

### 3.2 Model development and internal performance
LightGBM was the primary model selected a priori by internal cross-validated AUROC (＝2.8). Internal hold-out AUROC was **0.832 (95% CI 0.823?0.841)**, AUPRC 0.233, Brier 0.031, calibration slope 1.00, CITL +0.10 ? **H1 met**.

### 3.3 Geographic and temporal (COVID) external validation
See Table 2 and Figures 2 and 3. Discrimination remained clinically useful but lower: pre **0.772 (0.735?0.808)**, post **0.769 (0.739?0.799)**. Calibration deteriorated in both directions of the calibration line: the slope fell to 0.66 (pre) and 0.68 (post), and CITL rose to +0.49 and +1.11, indicating that the Korea-trained model systematically *under*-predicted absolute risk in the US?as expected from the large prevalence difference (KCHS 3.6% vs NHANES 8.4% and 12.2%). Out-of-sample intercept/slope recalibration restored calibration (slope⊥0.99, CITL⊥0.00) with discrimination essentially unchanged ? **H2 and H3 supported**.

**Table 2. Discrimination and calibration (primary model, LightGBM, 17 predictors, weighted).**

| Metric | Internal | NHANES pre | pre (recal.) | NHANES post | post (recal.) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| AUROC | 0.832 | 0.772 | 0.769 | 0.769 | 0.767 |
| 95% CI | 0.823?0.841 | 0.735?0.808 | ? | 0.739?0.799 | ? |
| AUPRC | 0.233 | 0.267 | 0.262 | 0.346 | 0.346 |
| 95% CI | 0.216?0.254 | 0.211?0.332 | ? | 0.307?0.390 | ? |
| Brier | 0.031 | 0.071 | 0.070 | 0.099 | 0.093 |
| ECE | 0.004 | 0.032 | ? | 0.067 | ? |
| Calibration slope | 1.00 | 0.66 | 0.99 | 0.68 | 0.99 |
