## Table S3. NHANES in-domain oracle by predictor set

Fit and cross-validated within NHANES on its own labels (5-fold, pooled pre+post; maximum of penalized-logistic and gradient-boosting learners). This estimates the best in-domain performance obtained by the learners tested when fitted directly to NHANES. It is an empirical benchmark over those learners, not a proof of a maximum.

| Predictor set | Pooled oracle AUROC | ЅФ vs 17 |
|---|---|---|
| 17 harmonized predictors | 0.765 | ? |
| 17 + insurance coverage | 0.766 | +0.000 |
| 17 + multimorbidity count | 0.774 | +0.009 |
| 17 + healthcare utilization (visits, hospitalization) | 0.771 | +0.006 |
| 17 + multimorbidity + utilization + insurance | **0.778** | **+0.012** |

The transported LightGBM model (pre 0.772, post 0.769) is at the level of the 17-predictor oracle, indicating the external number is not obviously below what these predictors support within NHANES itself.

### Table S3b. Oracle by learner, with confidence intervals

Pooled pre+post NHANES, fitted and 5-fold cross-validated on its own labels; 95% CIs from the rescaled stratified cluster bootstrap (1000 replicates, PSUs resampled within period-specific design strata). An in-sample (apparent) bound is not reported because it is strongly learner-dependent and carries no information about the ceiling. Table S3 above uses a fixed penalized-logistic/gradient-boosting pair to compare *predictor sets* on a common footing; it is not the learner maximum reported here, and the two should not be read as one sequence.

| Learner | Oracle AUROC (5-fold CV) | 95% CI |
|---|---|---|
| Logistic regression | 0.765 | 0.741?0.789 |
| Penalized logistic (L2, C=0.1) | 0.766 | 0.741?0.790 |
| Histogram gradient boosting | 0.767 | 0.743?0.792 |
| LightGBM (small) | 0.772 | 0.749?0.795 |

## Interpretation

- The 17-predictor external ceiling (~0.77) held against every leakage-controlled attempt we made, and the transported model already matches the best in-domain performance among the learners tested. Concept shift is the most consistent explanation, but it cannot be separated here from differences in how several predictors are measured, from development-survey coverage, or from complete-case selection; the single density-ratio correction we ran shows only that that procedure did not help.
- Multimorbidity and healthcare utilization carry additional, transportable signal in NHANES (oracle 0.765Ёц0.778). These domains are **not collected by KCHS** (only hypertension and diabetes are recorded as physician-diagnosed conditions across all survey years, both already included), so they cannot enter a KCHS-developed transportable model. Part of the residual ceiling is therefore development-survey coverage, not concept shift alone.

*Multimorbidity count comprised physician-diagnosed asthma, arthritis, thyroid disorder, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, liver disease, and cancer (circulatory conditions excluded per the prespecified predictor policy; mental-health service contact excluded as tautological). Healthcare-visit count (HUQ051) is unavailable in the NHANES August 2021?2023 file and was carried as pre-cycle only.*
--- MODEL CARD
# Model Card ? KCHSЁцNHANES Depression Screening Model (17-predictor)

## Model details
- **Model**: Gradient-boosting classifier (LightGBM) predicting current depression (PHQ-9 ЁУ10).
- **Version**: v2 (17-predictor), 2026. Supersedes the 14-predictor v1 for the expanded-predictor analysis; the 14-predictor model is retained separately.
- **Inputs (17)**: age, sex, education, household income (within-survey quintile), self-rated health, partnership, living alone, smoking, current drinking, physical-activity recommendation met, sleep hours, BMI, hypertension, diabetes, employment, self-rated oral health, food insecurity.
- **Output**: probability of PHQ-9 ЁУ10 (screen-positive risk).
- **Selection/tuning**: hyperparameters, feature representation, and algorithm all chosen by KCHS-internal cross-validation only (no external data used in any selection step). Selected config: num_leaves 31, min_child_samples 1000, learning_rate 0.01, n_estimators 900, reg_alpha 1.0, reg_lambda 20, colsample_bytree 0.6, subsample 0.6 (inactive ? subsample_freq left at its default of 0, so no row subsampling occurs). Chosen from a 24-configuration randomized search ЁП 2 feature representations by 4-fold survey-weighted CV (300,000-record subsample); algorithms compared at each one's tuned best under an identical 5-fold protocol (200,000-record subsample): LightGBM 0.840 > random forest 0.835 > elastic net 0.822.

## Intended use
- **Purpose**: population-level screening/triage aid to prioritize higher-risk individuals for further assessment, deliverable via a low-burden web/mobile self-report form.
- **Intended users**: public-health and primary-care screening programs; researchers.
- **Out of scope**: NOT a diagnostic device; not for standalone clinical decisions; not validated for individual-level clinical determination, for populations outside community-dwelling adults ЁУ20 years, or without local recalibration.

## Factors
- Evaluated across sex, age band, education (KCHS) and race/ethnicity (NHANES). Performance is lower in younger adults (20?39 y) and in some US minority subgroups (see Metrics).

## Metrics (complex-survey weighted)
| Setting | AUROC (95% CI) | Calibration slope | After recalibration |
|---|---|---|---|
| Internal (KCHS hold-out) | 0.832 (0.823?0.841) | 1.00 (CITL +0.10) | ? |
| NHANES pre-COVID | 0.772 (0.735?0.808) | 0.66 (CITL +0.49) | slopeЁц0.99, CITLЁц0.00 |
| NHANES post-COVID | 0.769 (0.739?0.799) | 0.68 (CITL +1.11) | slopeЁц0.99, CITLЁц0.00 |
- Parsimonious variant selected by the analysis plan's margin, entirely inside the training data: **5 items** (self-rated health, age, sex, sleep duration, oral health) ? hold-out 0.809, external 0.742 (pre) and 0.746 (post). A 7-item variant chosen under a stricter margin performs better externally (0.819/0.760/0.758) but that comparison inspected the external cohorts and is exploratory.
- CIs from a rescaled stratified cluster bootstrap (n_h?1 PSUs resampled within each design stratum with weight rescaling, 1000 replicates). CITL is the offset-model intercept; positive = under-prediction of observed risk.
- Operating point (Youden, computed over all observed scores): sensitivity/specificity 0.730/0.780 (internal), 0.733/0.687 (pre), 0.739/0.667 (post); PPV low (0.110?0.236), NPV high (0.948?0.987). At a triage-oriented sensitivity of 0.85 the number needed to screen is 7.1 (pre) and 5.4 (post).
- Subgroup AUROC: KCHS 0.785?0.859; NHANES race/ethnicity 0.689?0.841 across both cycles, with wide and mutually overlapping CIs (Mexican American lowest in both cycles).

## Training data
- KCHS 2017?2025, community-dwelling adults ЁУ20 y with complete PHQ-9; pooled n=2,048,510; weighted depression prevalence 3.6%. Complex-sample (stratified, clustered, weighted). The model was fitted on an outcome-stratified random 500,000-record training set, with a separate 100,000-record hold-out used for internal metrics.

## Evaluation data
- NHANES pre-pandemic (2017?March 2020; n=7,888; prevalence 8.4%) and post-pandemic (2021?2023; n=5,210; prevalence 12.2%). Applied without refitting.

## Ethical considerations
- Public, de-identified secondary data; no re-identification. Low PPV means many screen-positives are not depressed ? must be paired with confirmatory assessment and support pathways, not automated action. Subgroup performance differences (age, race/ethnicity) require monitoring to avoid inequitable triage.

## Caveats and recommendations
- **Deploy only with local recalibration**: discrimination transports, but calibration drifts across country and pandemic; intercept/slope recalibration on local data restores it.
- **Cross-national ceiling ~0.77**: external discrimination did not exceed ~0.77 in any leakage-controlled attempt, and an in-domain oracle fitted directly to NHANES reached only 0.765?0.772 against ~0.83 within Korea. Concept shift is the most consistent explanation, but differing measurement, development-survey coverage and complete-case selection cannot be separated from it in these data.
- **Food insecurity** is the most weakly harmonized predictor (single item vs 10-item module; 4?5ЁП prevalence difference); results are robust to its removal (16-predictor model).
- Self-reported inputs; cross-sectional (screens current symptoms, not future onset).
- Reproducibility: `17var_model/20_final_pipeline.py` (development and validation), `36_utility.py` (clinical utility), `47_review2_recompute.py` and `54_review3_ci.py` (current confidence intervals), `48_parsimonious_clean.py` (parsimonious selection), `49_continuous_score.py`, `51_sap_compliance.py`, `53_review3_fixes.py`. Current results are `results/review2_*.csv`, `review3_*.csv`, `parsimonious_clean.csv`, `shap_importance_train.csv`, `characteristics_availablecase.csv` and `table_s4_*.csv`; earlier result generations are retained under `results/_superseded/` and must not be quoted.
--- COVER LETTER
# Cover Letter ? Submission to the Journal of Medical Internet Research (JMIR)

*[Date] ЁЄ To the Editor-in-Chief, Journal of Medical Internet Research*

Dear Editor,

We are pleased to submit our Original Paper, **"A Machine Learning Depression Screener Developed in the Korea Community Health Survey and Externally Validated in the US National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey Across the COVID-19 Period: Development and External Validation Study,"** for consideration in JMIR.

**What the study does.** We develop a machine-learning depression screener (PHQ-9 ЁУ10) in the Korea Community Health Survey (n?2.05 million) and externally validate it?without refitting?in the US NHANES across NCHS-defined pre- and post-COVID-19 cycles. This is, to our knowledge, among the first depression-screening models to be validated across country, health system, and the pandemic distribution shift using an identically defined outcome in both surveys.

**Why it matters for JMIR's readership.** Most published survey-based depression models report internal validation only and, in Korean data, achieve internal AUROCs up to ~0.90 while relying on predictors (e.g., perceived stress, depressed-mood items) that are near-tautological with the outcome. Our study instead asks the question that matters for any deployable digital screener: does it transport? We find that discrimination remains clinically useful but lower on external data (AUROC ~0.77), that calibration drifts but is fully recoverable by lightweight intercept/slope recalibration, and?critically?that this ~0.77 external ceiling withstood every leakage-controlled attempt to raise it, including matching sample size, correcting covariate shift and fitting NHANES directly ? pointing to a limit of the transportable predictor set rather than a modeling failure. We establish this rigorously with an in-domain oracle (fitting NHANES on its own labels caps at ~0.77, whereas the same predictors reach ~0.83 within Korea) and >450 leakage-controlled optimization experiments, and we show that predictor-importance rank order is highly concordant across surveys (Spearman Ѕё?0.97?0.99). A parsimonious 5- to 7-item version retains most of the external discrimination, supporting a low-burden web/mobile triage tool?squarely within JMIR's scope of internet-based health services.

**Methodological rigor and transparency.** The work follows TRIPOD+AI (2024; checklist enclosed as Multimedia Appendix 1) with PROBAST risk-of-bias self-assessment. We report a predictor-set amendment (14Ёц17 predictors) that was documented and locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted ? though after the 14-predictor external result was known, which we state explicitly ? with the added predictors chosen on survey availability and non-circularity rather than on any outcome association; all model fitting, tuning, algorithm selection, and the choice of deployment item count used development-data cross-validation only, and the 14-predictor comparator was given its own hyperparameter search so the comparison is not confounded by tuning effort. We also disclose and correct three silent data-processing defects found in an adversarial data-integrity audit. The analysis code, variable-harmonization dictionary, model card, and protocol/pre-specification documents will be deposited in a public repository on acceptance; generative AI tools assisted with code and drafting under full author verification.

**Declarations.** This manuscript is original, not under consideration elsewhere, and all authors approve submission. We declare no competing interests. The study uses public, de-identified secondary data; raw KCHS microdata are access-controlled and not redistributable, while NHANES data are openly available.

We believe this study will interest JMIR readers working on scalable digital mental-health screening and on the real-world transportability of clinical AI. We suggest reviewers with expertise in clinical prediction modeling, survey epidemiology, and digital mental health, and have no reviewers to exclude.

Thank you for your consideration.

Sincerely,
[Corresponding author name, degrees, affiliation, email ? to complete]
on behalf of all authors
