docs\manuscript_17var.md-80-### 2.11 Interpretability, fairness, deployment
docs\manuscript_17var.md-81-SHAP global importance [9] and cross-survey rank concordance of predictor importance (Spearman correlation between KCHS and each NHANES cycle) as a mechanistic view of transportability; fairness by weighted subgroup AUROC and calibration slope across sex, age, education, and (NHANES) race/ethnicity; and a parsimonious model for a low-burden self-administered form. Both the predictor order and the item count were determined entirely inside the development training set, which was split into a 450,000-record fitting set and a 50,000-record selection set: predictors were entered in descending order of global SHAP importance computed on a random 20,000-record subsample of the fitting set, refitted at every item count from 1 to 17, and the item count was chosen on selection-set AUROC. The item count follows the analysis plan's margin — the smallest set within 0.02 AUROC of the full model, subject to a 5–10 item ceiling; a variant using a stricter 0.01 margin is reported alongside it and flagged as post hoc (§3.6). The internal hold-out and both NHANES cohorts were used only for evaluation (§3.6). Positioned as a screening/triage aid, not a diagnostic.
docs\manuscript_17var.md-82-
docs\manuscript_17var.md-83-### 2.12 Sensitivity analyses
docs\manuscript_17var.md:84:Pre-specified and additional analyses (exact values in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7): outcome cut-off (≥5 as well as ≥10, evaluated internally and in both external cohorts), removal of the three added predictors (→14), removal of food insecurity only (→16), weighting approach (weighted vs unweighted training), alternative imputation (missing indicators for continuous and binary predictors; single chained-equations imputation of continuous predictors), an age lower bound of 30 years applied to the training *and* evaluation samples, and—as pre-specified in the protocol—a continuous-score analysis evaluating how the model ranks the untransformed PHQ-9 total, and alternative definitions of the alcohol predictor (drinking at least twice weekly, binge drinking, or all three indicators together in place of current drinking). The self-report (KCHS) versus measured (NHANES) difference in BMI is inherent to the data sources and is addressed under harmonization cautions (§2.5) and Limitations rather than by swapping measurements. All analyses were performed in Python 3.14 (scikit-learn 1.9.0, lightgbm 4.6.0, shap 0.52.0, numpy 2.4.5, pandas 3.0.3, scipy 1.18.0, pyreadstat 1.3.5, matplotlib 3.10.9); complex-survey weighting, the stratified cluster bootstrap, calibration measures, and decision-curve net benefit were implemented directly rather than through a survey-analysis package, and the implementations are released with the code.
