145:On decision-curve analysis (weighted, with cohort-specific screen-all and screen-none default strategies), the model provided positive net benefit exceeding both defaults across a clinically relevant threshold-probability range in all three datasets (Figure 7). At the Youden-optimal threshold, computed over all observed scores rather than a coarse grid, sensitivity/specificity were 0.730/0.780 (internal), 0.733/0.687 (NHANES pre), and 0.739/0.667 (NHANES post); positive predictive value was low (0.110â€“0.236) and negative predictive value high (0.948â€“0.987), as expected for population screening at low prevalence (full operating characteristics across thresholds in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S1). Tuned to a triage-appropriate 85% sensitivity, the model flagged roughly half of the external population (pre 51%, post 55%) at a PPV of 0.14 (pre) and 0.19 (post)â€”a number needed to screen of 7.1 and 5.4â€”while the 5-item deployment model flagged somewhat more people for slightly lower yield (PPV 0.122 and 0.170; number needed to screen 8.2 and 5.9). After local intercept/slope recalibration, net benefit remained positive and exceeded both default strategiesâ€”screening everyone and screening no oneâ€”at threshold probabilities of 0.10â€“0.15 in every dataset; screen-all net benefit was in fact negative at these thresholds everywhere except NHANES post at 0.10, where the model still added +0.033 (Multimedia Appendix 3, Tables S4â€“S5). Clinical utility remains provisional pending prospective evaluation.
75:Discrimination (AUROC, AUPRC), calibration, and operating characteristics, all complex-surveyâ€“weighted. Calibration was summarized by the calibration slope and by calibration-in-the-large (CITL), the latter estimated as the intercept of a weighted logistic model with the linear predictor entered as an offset (ie, with the slope fixed at 1), together with the Brier score and expected calibration error (ECE); a positive CITL indicates that observed risk exceeds predicted risk. Ninety-five percent CIs are reported for AUROC and AUPRC only; calibration measures and operating characteristics are point estimates. CIs were obtained by a rescaled stratified cluster bootstrap with 1000 replicates, for cohort-level and subgroup estimates alike: within each design stratum, *n*<sub>h</sub>âˆ’1 primary sampling units were resampled with replacement and the survey weights rescaled by *n*<sub>h</sub>/(*n*<sub>h</sub>âˆ’1) times the selection count [16]. Rescaling is necessary because most NHANES strata contain only two primary sampling units, for which an ordinary within-stratum cluster bootstrap understates variance. We verified the implementation against a closed-form design-based (linearized ultimate-cluster) standard error for weighted depression prevalence: bootstrap 0.00466 vs design 0.00471 in the pre-pandemic cycle and 0.00802 vs 0.00796 in the post-pandemic cycle, i.e. agreement within 1%. For KCHS the stratum is defined as survey year crossed with the design stratum, because weights are normalized within year and stratum codes recur across years. Subgroup intervals are domain estimates: replicate weights are generated once on the full cohort design and then applied to each subgroup, rather than rebuilding the design after subsetting. Strata left with a single primary sampling unit are pooled into one pseudo-stratum so their variance contribution is not lost. Clinical utility by decision-curve analysis [8].
161:Cross-sectional (screens current symptoms, not future onset); differing measurement of alcohol, physical activity, income, BMI, and food insecurity; DPQ non-response of 14.3% (pre) and 32.7% (post) handled as complete-case and differential by education and race/ethnicity (Â§3.1), which may bias the external estimatesâ€”especially the post-pandemic subgroup resultsâ€”in an unknown direction; living-alone unavailable in NHANES pre; food insecurity single-item and unmeasured in KCHS 2020â€“2021; reduced discrimination in some racial/ethnic subgroups (wide CIs); low PPV at population prevalence (triage aid, not diagnostic); and prior depression diagnosis not recorded in KCHS 2017â€“2025 (precluding a diagnosis-exclusion sensitivity analysis). Model development used a 500,000-record training set and a 100,000-record hold-out drawn from the 2,048,510 pooled records rather than the full pooled sample (Â§2.2); a training-set size experiment showed this did not cost external discrimination (Â§3.5), but internal estimates are hold-out rather than fully cross-validated. The 14-predictor comparator was trained with the same algorithm and a separately tuned configuration (Â§3.4), yet both models share a single preprocessing and evaluation pipeline, so the 14â†’17 comparison isolates predictor content rather than modeling strategy. The internal hold-out was drawn by simple random split, so 92.0% of its records belong to primary sampling units also represented in the training set; the internal estimate therefore describes new individuals from already-sampled clusters rather than wholly new clusters, and is expected to be mildly optimistic relative to a cluster-level split. The predictor-set amendment, although locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted, was written after the 14-predictor external result was known (Â§2.5.1). H2 and H4 were stated qualitatively in the protocol, without numeric decision margins, so judging them 'supported' involves interpretation. Finally, item reduction should be re-evaluated whenever the deployment population differs materially from the development population: adding smoking as an eighth item helps the pre-pandemic cohort but hurts the post-pandemic one (Â§3.6).
