86:Candidates were drawn from a crosswalk of all 616 KCHS concepts against the NHANES codebook for both cycles (a 175-variable extract of candidate items at the time of the amendment; a later, exhaustive extraction identified 265 variables common to both cycles). A candidate was eligible only if it was (i) available in both NHANES cycles, (ii) collected in more than one KCHS survey year, and (iii) not circular with the PHQ-9 outcome. These criteria use survey documentation and variable availability only; no NHANES outcome data were consulted.
61:**Post-hoc candidate screening (reported for completeness).** The later exhaustive extraction surfaced further harmonizable items, some of which would also have satisfied criteria (i)–(iii)—notably public-assistance receipt, alcohol frequency, alcohol quantity, binge drinking, and weekend sleep duration; the alcohol and sleep items are refinements of predictors already in the set. After the primary analysis was complete we screened all of them, comparing the direction of their adjusted odds ratios with depression across KCHS, NHANES-pre, and NHANES-post, and then testing them in the model. Alcohol quantity, binge drinking, and weekend sleep reversed direction between countries; public-assistance receipt was directionally consistent but added no external discrimination when included (ΔAUROC <0.001; §3.5). No item was added, so the predictor set is unchanged from the locked amendment. Because this screening inspected NHANES outcome associations, it is reported as a post-hoc diagnostic and was not used for selection. Two robustness analyses further bound the amendment's contribution: the 14-predictor protocol-specified model is reported alongside the 17-predictor model throughout, and a food-insecurity leave-out (16-predictor) shows the incremental gain does not rest on the most weakly harmonized predictor (§3.4). Model fitting, hyperparameter tuning, and primary-model selection used KCHS-internal cross-validation only, with no NHANES data.
13:**Methodological rigor and transparency.** The work follows TRIPOD+AI (2024; checklist enclosed as Multimedia Appendix 1) with PROBAST risk-of-bias self-assessment. We report a predictor-set amendment (14→17 predictors) that was documented and locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted — though after the 14-predictor external result was known, which we state explicitly — with the added predictors chosen on survey availability and non-circularity rather than on any outcome association; all model fitting, tuning, algorithm selection, and the choice of deployment item count used development-data cross-validation only, and the 14-predictor comparator was given its own hyperparameter search so the comparison is not confounded by tuning effort. We also disclose and correct three silent data-processing defects found in an adversarial data-integrity audit. The analysis code, variable-harmonization dictionary, model card, and protocol/pre-specification documents will be deposited in a public repository on acceptance; generative AI tools assisted with code and drafting under full author verification.
docs\cover_letter.md:11:**Why it matters for JMIR's readership.** Most published survey-based depression models report internal validation only and, in Korean data, achieve internal AUROCs up to ~0.90 while relying on predictors (e.g., perceived stress, depressed-mood items) that are near-tautological with the outcome. Our study instead asks the question that matters for any deployable digital screener: does it transport? We find that discrimination remains clinically useful but lower on external data (AUROC ~0.77), that calibration drifts but is fully recoverable by lightweight intercept/slope recalibration, and—critically—that this ~0.77 external ceiling withstood every leakage-controlled attempt to raise it, including matching sample size, correcting covariate shift and fitting NHANES directly — pointing to a limit of the transportable predictor set rather than a modeling failure. We establish this rigorously with an in-domain oracle (fitting NHANES on its own labels caps at ~0.77, whereas the same predictors reach ~0.83 within Korea) and >450 leakage-controlled optimization experiments, and we show that predictor-importance rank order is highly concordant across surveys (Spearman ρ≈0.97–0.99). A parsimonious 5- to 7-item version retains most of the external discrimination, supporting a low-burden web/mobile triage tool—squarely within JMIR's scope of internet-based health services.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:21:**Conclusions.** A Korea-developed depression screener transported to the US and across the pandemic with clinically useful discrimination and fully recoverable calibration. The cross-national ceiling (~0.77) persisted against sample size, algorithm choice, covariate-shift correction and an in-domain oracle, and is therefore a property of the transportable predictor set rather than a modeling failure. A low-burden 5- to 7-item version, paired with local recalibration, is a plausible scalable digital triage aid.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:166:A survey-derived depression screener transports across country and pandemic with clinically useful discrimination and recoverable calibration. The cross-national discrimination ceiling (~0.77) is a property of the transportable predictor set—most consistent with shift in the predictor–outcome relationship, with residual contributions from differing measurement, development-survey coverage and complete-case selection—rather than a modeling failure. A low-burden 5- to 7-item version, paired with local recalibration, is a plausible scalable digital triage aid.
137:SHAP values summarize global and local predictor contributions, and the rank concordance of top predictors between KCHS and NHANES is examined as a mechanistic view of transportability. Fairness is assessed by reporting AUROC, calibration, and error rates across sex, age, education, income, and (in NHANES) race/ethnicity. For deployment, a parsimonious model using the top 5–10 predictors is derived to minimize item burden in a self-administered web/mobile form; a conceptual triage workflow routes low-risk users to self-management resources and higher-risk users to professional assessment. The tool is positioned as a screening/triage aid, not a standalone diagnostic or clinical-decision system.
