docs\manuscript_17var.md:19:**Results.** Gradient boosting (LightGBM) was the primary model. Internal AUROC was 0.832 (95% CI 0.823–0.841), meeting the ≥0.80 target (H1). External discrimination remained clinically useful but lower (pre 0.772, 95% CI 0.735–0.808; post 0.769, 0.739–0.799); calibration deteriorated (slope 0.66–0.68; calibration-in-the-large +0.49 and +1.11, ie, under-prediction) and was fully restored by intercept/slope recalibration (slope→0.99, CITL→0.00; H2, H3 supported). Against an independently tuned 14-predictor model, the three added predictors improved external discrimination (pre 0.749→0.772, Δ+0.023, 95% CI +0.013 to +0.033; post 0.753→0.769, Δ+0.016, +0.008 to +0.023; both *P*<.001). Applying the analysis plan's parsimony margin, with predictor order and item count fixed inside the development data, selected a 5-item model (0.809 internal, 0.742 pre, 0.746 post; H4 partially supported); a 7-item variant transports better (0.819/0.760/0.758) but was chosen post hoc. Leakage-controlled optimization (>450 experiments) did not exceed ~0.77 externally, and an oracle fitted directly to NHANES reached only 0.765–0.772, whereas the same predictors reached 0.81–0.82 in equally sized Korean samples.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:21:**Conclusions.** A Korea-developed depression screener transported to the US and across the pandemic with clinically useful discrimination and fully recoverable calibration. The cross-national ceiling (~0.77) persisted against sample size, algorithm choice, covariate-shift correction and an in-domain oracle, and is therefore a property of the transportable predictor set rather than a modeling failure. A low-burden 5- to 7-item version, paired with local recalibration, is a plausible scalable digital triage aid.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:59:**2.5.1 Predictor-set amendment and selection provenance.** The three added predictors were identified by crosswalking all 616 KCHS concepts against the NHANES codebook for both cycles (a 175-variable extract of candidate items at the time of the amendment; a later, exhaustive extraction identified 265 variables common to both cycles). The amendment was locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted, but not before the study as a whole: the 14-predictor model had already been developed and transferred, and the locked document explicitly records the then-known internal AUROC (0.824) and the then-observed external level (~0.75) when stating its expectations (Multimedia Appendix 2). The expansion decision therefore could not be tuned on 17-predictor external results, but it was not blind to how the 14-predictor model had performed externally, and we report it as a documented amendment rather than as a blinded pre-registration. The amendment required a candidate to be (i) available in both NHANES cycles, (ii) collected in more than one KCHS survey year, and (iii) not circular with the PHQ-9 outcome. Employment, self-rated oral health, and household food insecurity were the three concepts adopted. Candidates rejected at this stage failed on single-year collection (e.g., dyslipidemia, osteoporosis, arthritis), concept mismatch (KCHS records only *awareness of early warning signs* for stroke and myocardial infarction, not physician diagnosis), or absence from the NHANES pre-pandemic cycle (functional limitation). Criteria (i)–(iii) themselves use survey documentation and variable availability only, and no NHANES outcome association was estimated for any candidate before the amendment was locked. Each added predictor has an established association with depression in the epidemiological literature—food insecurity (pooled odds ratio ≈1.4) [13], unemployment (≈1.9–2.1) [14], and poor oral health (a bidirectional relationship) [15]—supporting biological and social plausibility rather than data-driven selection. Circular/outcome-contaminated variables (PHQ items, suicidal ideation, EQ-5D anxiety/depression, happiness, perceived stress, mental-health service use including NHANES HUQ090) were pre-specified for exclusion.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:61:**Post-hoc candidate screening (reported for completeness).** The later exhaustive extraction surfaced further harmonizable items, some of which would also have satisfied criteria (i)–(iii)—notably public-assistance receipt, alcohol frequency, alcohol quantity, binge drinking, and weekend sleep duration; the alcohol and sleep items are refinements of predictors already in the set. After the primary analysis was complete we screened all of them, comparing the direction of their adjusted odds ratios with depression across KCHS, NHANES-pre, and NHANES-post, and then testing them in the model. Alcohol quantity, binge drinking, and weekend sleep reversed direction between countries; public-assistance receipt was directionally consistent but added no external discrimination when included (ΔAUROC <0.001; §3.5). No item was added, so the predictor set is unchanged from the locked amendment. Because this screening inspected NHANES outcome associations, it is reported as a post-hoc diagnostic and was not used for selection. Two robustness analyses further bound the amendment's contribution: the 14-predictor protocol-specified model is reported alongside the 17-predictor model throughout, and a food-insecurity leave-out (16-predictor) shows the incremental gain does not rest on the most weakly harmonized predictor (§3.4). Model fitting, hyperparameter tuning, and primary-model selection used KCHS-internal cross-validation only, with no NHANES data.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:72:Algorithms: penalized logistic regression (elastic net), random forest, and gradient boosting (LightGBM). Development proceeded in two selection steps, both confined to the KCHS training set. First, the gradient-boosting hyperparameters and the feature representation were chosen by a randomized search over 24 LightGBM configurations crossed with two representations—main effects only, versus main effects plus four candidate interaction terms, specified when the search was written but not named in any earlier document (age×self-rated health, self-rated health×oral health, age×employment, income×employment)—evaluated by 4-fold survey-weighted cross-validation in a 300,000-record subsample of the training set; the main-effects-only representation won, and adding interaction terms did not improve cross-validated discrimination. Second, all three algorithms were tuned over comparable 5-configuration grids under an identical protocol (5-fold stratified cross-validation in a 200,000-record subsample of the training set, scored by unweighted cross-validated AUROC) and compared at each algorithm's best configuration, so that no algorithm was advantaged by differential tuning effort. The analysis plan fixed the primary-model rule in advance: highest cross-validated AUROC, with differences smaller than 0.005 treated as ties and broken by proximity of the calibration slope to 1, and only then by parsimony. The pipeline as executed ranked the algorithms directly on AUROC and then calibration slope without constructing the tie band, so the plan's rule was checked retrospectively. **No NHANES data were used in any selection or tuning step.** Complex-survey weights were handled by unweighted training with weighted evaluation (probed in sensitivity). No class weighting, resampling, or other imbalance correction was applied; the learner was fitted on the natural 3.6% event rate, which departs from the plan's recommended class weighting (Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S11). LightGBM (0.840) and random forest (0.835) differ by 0.0046 and therefore fall inside the tie band, so the plan's tie-break applies; it selects LightGBM, whose cross-validated calibration slope was 1.003 against 1.203 for random forest. Elastic net (0.822) lies outside the band and the parsimony criterion is not reached, so the retrospective check reproduces the model the pipeline selected (Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S11). LightGBM was refitted on the full 500,000-record training set; the selected hyperparameters and the full search grids are reported in the model card, which is deposited with the code (see Data Availability). The selected row-subsampling fraction is inactive in the deployed configuration because the subsampling frequency was left at its default of zero, so the fitted model uses all rows at every boosting iteration.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:75:Discrimination (AUROC, AUPRC), calibration, and operating characteristics, all complex-survey–weighted. Calibration was summarized by the calibration slope and by calibration-in-the-large (CITL), the latter estimated as the intercept of a weighted logistic model with the linear predictor entered as an offset (ie, with the slope fixed at 1), together with the Brier score and expected calibration error (ECE); a positive CITL indicates that observed risk exceeds predicted risk. Ninety-five percent CIs are reported for AUROC and AUPRC only; calibration measures and operating characteristics are point estimates. CIs were obtained by a rescaled stratified cluster bootstrap with 1000 replicates, for cohort-level and subgroup estimates alike: within each design stratum, *n*<sub>h</sub>−1 primary sampling units were resampled with replacement and the survey weights rescaled by *n*<sub>h</sub>/(*n*<sub>h</sub>−1) times the selection count [16]. Rescaling is necessary because most NHANES strata contain only two primary sampling units, for which an ordinary within-stratum cluster bootstrap understates variance. We verified the implementation against a closed-form design-based (linearized ultimate-cluster) standard error for weighted depression prevalence: bootstrap 0.00466 vs design 0.00471 in the pre-pandemic cycle and 0.00802 vs 0.00796 in the post-pandemic cycle, i.e. agreement within 1%. For KCHS the stratum is defined as survey year crossed with the design stratum, because weights are normalized within year and stratum codes recur across years. Subgroup intervals are domain estimates: replicate weights are generated once on the full cohort design and then applied to each subgroup, rather than rebuilding the design after subsetting. Strata left with a single primary sampling unit are pooled into one pseudo-stratum so their variance contribution is not lost. Clinical utility by decision-curve analysis [8].
docs\manuscript_17var.md:81:SHAP global importance [9] and cross-survey rank concordance of predictor importance (Spearman correlation between KCHS and each NHANES cycle) as a mechanistic view of transportability; fairness by weighted subgroup AUROC and calibration slope across sex, age, education, and (NHANES) race/ethnicity; and a parsimonious model for a low-burden self-administered form. Both the predictor order and the item count were determined entirely inside the development training set, which was split into a 450,000-record fitting set and a 50,000-record selection set: predictors were entered in descending order of global SHAP importance computed on a random 20,000-record subsample of the fitting set, refitted at every item count from 1 to 17, and the item count was chosen on selection-set AUROC. The item count follows the analysis plan's margin — the smallest set within 0.02 AUROC of the full model, subject to a 5–10 item ceiling; a variant using a stricter 0.01 margin is reported alongside it and flagged as post hoc (§3.6). The internal hold-out and both NHANES cohorts were used only for evaluation (§3.6). Positioned as a screening/triage aid, not a diagnostic.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:97:Weighted depression prevalence (PHQ-9 ≥10): KCHS 3.6%, NHANES pre 8.4%, post 12.2% (Figure 1). The surveys differed markedly on measured covariates—e.g., mean BMI 23.7 (KCHS) vs 29.8 (NHANES), and weighted food-insecurity prevalence 3.7% in KCHS vs 16.4% and 17.2% in the NHANES cycles, computed among respondents with the item recorded (missing 22.1%, 6.0% and 6.3%, respectively; every characteristic in Table S6 uses available-case denominators)—consistent with genuinely distinct populations and, for food insecurity, with the single-item-vs-module measurement difference. DPQ item non-response was substantial in both NHANES cycles and much larger after the pandemic: among adults ≥20 years, 14.3% of the pre-pandemic and 32.7% of the post-pandemic respondents left three or more PHQ-9 items unanswered, yielding the complete-case analytic samples of 7,888 and 5,210 (item-level detail in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S10). Non-response was differential—higher among respondents with less than high-school education (20.6% pre, 47.3% post) and among Asian and Black respondents—so the external cohorts, and particularly the post-pandemic fairness estimates, should be read as complete-case results. Reassuringly, adding respondents missing only one or two items using their person-mean changed the observed prevalence by ≤0.1 percentage points in both cycles (unweighted: 9.23%→9.27% pre, 12.69%→12.78% post). Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S6.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:100:LightGBM was the primary model selected a priori by internal cross-validated AUROC (§2.8). Internal hold-out AUROC was **0.832 (95% CI 0.823–0.841)**, AUPRC 0.233, Brier 0.031, calibration slope 1.00, CITL +0.10 — **H1 met**.
