docs\manuscript_17var.md:126:Extensive leakage-controlled optimization (>450 experiments: feature engineering, hyperparameter search, heterogeneous ensembling, transfer-robust internal selection, domain adaptation, alternative losses, and structural variants) did not exceed external AUROC ~0.77 when model selection was restricted to KCHS-internal criteria. Apparent gains from externally-tuned variants (e.g., specific interaction terms, ensemble weights) did not survive leakage control.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:128:We localized the ceiling with an **in-domain oracle**: fitting NHANES on its own labels (a deliberate §2.10 violation, for diagnosis only) under cross-validation. Across logistic, penalized-logistic, histogram-gradient-boosting and LightGBM learners, the honest 5-fold cross-validated oracle reached only 0.765–0.772 (best learner 0.772, 95% CI 0.749–0.795 by the same rescaled cluster bootstrap). The KCHS-transferred model (pre 0.772, post 0.769) therefore already **matches the best in-domain performance any of these learners achieved when fitted directly to NHANES**. This is an empirical benchmark over the learners we tried, not a proof of a maximum; it is estimated on the pooled pre+post sample whereas the transported figures are cycle-specific, and its interval overlaps the transported estimates, so the comparison is approximate and cannot exclude a modest true gap. Matching sample size does not close the gap: reducing the Korean data to the NHANES sample size and cross-validating within it still yielded 0.823 at n=7,888 (the pre-pandemic cohort size) and 0.810 at n=13,098 (the pooled oracle size), rising to 0.830 only at n=200,000 (best of three learners, 5-fold weighted AUROC). At identical n, in other words, the same 17 predictors carry 0.81–0.82 of discrimination in Korea against an NHANES ceiling of ~0.77. Three further checks converged on the same conclusion: (i) covariate-shift correction by density-ratio reweighting of the KCHS training sample toward the NHANES covariate distribution (labels unused) *reduced* external AUROC (to 0.766/0.758), indicating the gap is not covariate shift; (ii) increasing the KCHS training set from 0.5M to 2M examples did not raise—and slightly lowered—external discrimination, consistent with source-specific overfitting rather than an estimation-limited ceiling; and (iii) adding the remaining harmonized-but-unused survey items did not help: public-assistance receipt alone left external AUROC unchanged (0.772/0.769), and adding all nine remaining candidates together slightly *reduced* it (−0.003 pre, −0.001 post in the exploration run). Deep tabular learners (a multilayer perceptron, TabNet, and an FT-Transformer) were additionally trained and transferred; all fell below gradient boosting internally and none exceeded it on both external cohorts (Multimedia Appendix 4), consistent with the model-agnostic ceiling. Sample size, model choice, covariate imbalance and omitted harmonized information were each tested and none accounts for the gap. The most consistent remaining explanation is **shift in the predictor–outcome relationship itself**—concept shift—although the surveys also differ in how several predictors are measured (§2.5) and the external cohorts are complete-case samples with differential depression-module non-response (§3.1), so measurement and selection effects cannot be separated from concept shift with these data. What the evidence does establish is that the gap is not closable by recalibration, which corrects calibration rather than rank ordering, nor by more data or a different learner.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:138:Discrimination was stable across specifications that alter modeling choices rather than the target condition (Figure 4): weighted training (0.831/0.773/0.764 internal/pre/post), missing-indicator imputation (0.833/0.770/0.767), single chained-equations imputation of the continuous predictors (0.833/0.770/0.767), and the 16- and 14-predictor variants (§3.4) all lay within 0.01 of the primary model internally. Restricting both training and evaluation to respondents aged ≥30 years *improved* discrimination (0.840/0.784/0.788), consistent with the lower subgroup performance observed at ages 20–39 (§3.8). Changing the outcome definition to PHQ-9 ≥5 lowered discrimination throughout (0.759/0.736/0.739) at a much higher weighted prevalence (16.1%/25.0%/32.1%), as expected when the target shifts from moderate-or-worse symptoms to any symptom burden; notably, the Korea–US gap narrows from about 0.06 AUROC at the ≥10 threshold to about 0.02 at ≥5 (0.759 vs 0.736–0.739), suggesting that the transportability loss is concentrated at the more severe end of the symptom distribution. Replacing current drinking with drinking at least twice weekly, with binge drinking, or entering all three indicators together — the alternative alcohol definitions listed in the analysis plan — changed discrimination by at most 0.003 externally (pre 0.769–0.772, post 0.769–0.770). In the pre-specified continuous-score analysis, the deployed classifier ranked the untransformed PHQ-9 total with weighted Spearman ρ=0.378 internally, 0.403 pre-pandemic and 0.439 post-pandemic, and a model trained directly on the continuous score performed similarly (ρ=0.402/0.403/0.424; weighted R² 0.216/0.166/0.102), so the transportability pattern is not an artifact of dichotomizing the outcome. Data-integrity fixes (§2.13) did not alter substantive conclusions. Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:141:Global SHAP importance, computed in the development fitting partition, was dominated by self-rated health (mean |SHAP| 0.57), followed by age (0.38), sex (0.29), sleep duration (0.26), **self-rated oral health (0.23)**, **employment (0.17)**, and partnership (0.16); food insecurity contributed modestly (0.06, ranked 10th). Two of the three added predictors (oral health, employment) thus rank within the top six, consistent with their measured incremental value (Figure 5). The rank ordering of predictor importance was highly concordant between KCHS and both NHANES cycles (Spearman ρ=0.97 pre, 0.99 post), indicating that the two populations use the predictors in a similar order; this suggests the transportability limit lies in the *strength*, not the *structure*, of predictor–outcome associations. The clearest exception was food insecurity, which ranked 10th in KCHS but 7th in NHANES pre, consistent with its different measurement (single item vs 10-item module) and much higher prevalence in the US.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:143:Subgroup discrimination in KCHS was fairly uniform (weighted AUROC 0.785–0.859): sex (female 0.822, 95% CI 0.811–0.834; male 0.831, 0.814–0.847), education (0.813–0.830), and age (20–39 years 0.785, 0.764–0.807 < 40–59 years 0.828, 0.811–0.845 < ≥60 years 0.859, 0.847–0.871). In NHANES, discrimination was lower and far less precisely estimated. Pre-pandemic: Mexican American 0.697 (0.631–0.770; n=914), non-Hispanic Black 0.762 (0.729–0.804; n=2,096), other/multiracial 0.772 (0.679–0.868; n=381), other Hispanic 0.777 (0.736–0.817; n=813), non-Hispanic White 0.778 (0.731–0.822; n=2,798), non-Hispanic Asian 0.810 (0.719–0.874; n=886). Post-pandemic: Mexican American 0.689 (0.538–0.805; n=330), non-Hispanic Black 0.727 (0.646–0.788; n=598), non-Hispanic White 0.770 (0.723–0.811; n=3,215), other/multiracial 0.786 (0.701–0.863; n=340), other Hispanic 0.790 (0.751–0.845; n=499), non-Hispanic Asian 0.841 (0.651–0.988; n=228). Applying the fairness flag set in the analysis plan — a subgroup AUROC differing from the cohort value by more than 0.05 — only Mexican American respondents are flagged, in both cycles (−0.075 pre, −0.080 post). These intervals are wide and mutually overlapping, however, and every one of them overlaps the cohort-level interval, so the ordering should not be read as established differential performance; that the lowest point estimate falls in Mexican American respondents in both cycles nonetheless warrants monitoring as an equity caveat (Figure 6). Internal subgroup calibration was good (slope 0.97–1.02 across sex and age strata, 0.93–1.03 including education strata; subgroup CITL −0.02 to +0.26). In NHANES the subgroup calibration slopes were uniformly below 1 (0.55–0.78, one exception) and subgroup CITL was positive in every group except Mexican American pre-pandemic (up to +1.30), confirming that the external miscalibration is under-prediction of absolute risk and not only over-extreme ranking (§3.3). Exact subgroup values with CIs are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S9.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:145:On decision-curve analysis (weighted, with cohort-specific screen-all and screen-none default strategies), the model provided positive net benefit exceeding both defaults across a clinically relevant threshold-probability range in all three datasets (Figure 7). At the Youden-optimal threshold, computed over all observed scores rather than a coarse grid, sensitivity/specificity were 0.730/0.780 (internal), 0.733/0.687 (NHANES pre), and 0.739/0.667 (NHANES post); positive predictive value was low (0.110–0.236) and negative predictive value high (0.948–0.987), as expected for population screening at low prevalence (full operating characteristics across thresholds in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S1). Tuned to a triage-appropriate 85% sensitivity, the model flagged roughly half of the external population (pre 51%, post 55%) at a PPV of 0.14 (pre) and 0.19 (post)—a number needed to screen of 7.1 and 5.4—while the 5-item deployment model flagged somewhat more people for slightly lower yield (PPV 0.122 and 0.170; number needed to screen 8.2 and 5.9). After local intercept/slope recalibration, net benefit remained positive and exceeded both default strategies—screening everyone and screening no one—at threshold probabilities of 0.10–0.15 in every dataset; screen-all net benefit was in fact negative at these thresholds everywhere except NHANES post at 0.10, where the model still added +0.033 (Multimedia Appendix 3, Tables S4–S5). Clinical utility remains provisional pending prospective evaluation.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:152:A depression-screening ML model developed in Korea and externally validated in the US met its internal target (AUROC 0.832) and retained clinically useful—though lower—discrimination (~0.77) on genuinely external US data and across the COVID-19 shift, with calibration recoverable by lightweight recalibration. Expanding the transportable predictor set from 14 to 17 produced a real external gain that survived giving the 14-predictor comparator its own hyperparameter search; further modeling did not, and the external plateau at ~0.77 survived every leakage-controlled attempt to raise it, including matching sample size, correcting covariate shift and fitting NHANES directly.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:155:Prior ML depression models built on Korean national surveys report strong internal performance but no external validation. Using KNHANES 2014–2018 restricted to non-smokers at risk of second-hand smoke exposure (n=11,463), Kim et al [12] classified PHQ-9 ≥10 with internal AUROC up to 0.90; using the 2023 KCHS restricted to culturally diverse families (n=2,568), Kim et al [11] predicted the continuous PHQ-9 score with a test R² of 33%—both identifying perceived stress and subjective health as dominant predictors, and neither attempting external validation. Two deliberate design choices explain why our internal discrimination is lower (0.83): we excluded outcome-proximal predictors such as perceived stress and single-item depressed-mood questions, which inflate apparent performance but are near-tautological with the outcome; and we restricted the predictor set to variables transportable to a second country. In exchange, this study provides what prior work did not—genuine cross-national and cross-pandemic external validation, an explicit transportability ceiling characterized by an in-domain oracle and >450 leakage-controlled experiments, and a recalibration analysis. That the same predictors carry substantially more signal within Korea (~0.83) than in the US (~0.77) is a concrete instance of the dataset-shift problem described for deployed clinical AI [10], and underscores that high internally-reported AUROCs do not guarantee cross-population usefulness.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:161:Cross-sectional (screens current symptoms, not future onset); differing measurement of alcohol, physical activity, income, BMI, and food insecurity; DPQ non-response of 14.3% (pre) and 32.7% (post) handled as complete-case and differential by education and race/ethnicity (§3.1), which may bias the external estimates—especially the post-pandemic subgroup results—in an unknown direction; living-alone unavailable in NHANES pre; food insecurity single-item and unmeasured in KCHS 2020–2021; reduced discrimination in some racial/ethnic subgroups (wide CIs); low PPV at population prevalence (triage aid, not diagnostic); and prior depression diagnosis not recorded in KCHS 2017–2025 (precluding a diagnosis-exclusion sensitivity analysis). Model development used a 500,000-record training set and a 100,000-record hold-out drawn from the 2,048,510 pooled records rather than the full pooled sample (§2.2); a training-set size experiment showed this did not cost external discrimination (§3.5), but internal estimates are hold-out rather than fully cross-validated. The 14-predictor comparator was trained with the same algorithm and a separately tuned configuration (§3.4), yet both models share a single preprocessing and evaluation pipeline, so the 14→17 comparison isolates predictor content rather than modeling strategy. The internal hold-out was drawn by simple random split, so 92.0% of its records belong to primary sampling units also represented in the training set; the internal estimate therefore describes new individuals from already-sampled clusters rather than wholly new clusters, and is expected to be mildly optimistic relative to a cluster-level split. The predictor-set amendment, although locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted, was written after the 14-predictor external result was known (§2.5.1). H2 and H4 were stated qualitatively in the protocol, without numeric decision margins, so judging them 'supported' involves interpretation. Finally, item reduction should be re-evaluated whenever the deployment population differs materially from the development population: adding smoking as an eighth item helps the pre-pandemic cohort but hurts the post-pandemic one (§3.6).
docs\manuscript_17var.md:163:The transportable predictor set is also bounded by the *development* survey's content. In a coverage analysis we extracted candidate domains available in NHANES but not harmonizable to KCHS—physician-diagnosed multimorbidity (asthma, arthritis, thyroid, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, liver disease, and cancer, excluding circulatory conditions) and healthcare utilization—and found that adding them raised the NHANES in-domain oracle from 0.765 to 0.778. KCHS, however, records only hypertension and diabetes among physician-diagnosed conditions (both already included) and does not collect a broader chronic-disease inventory, hospitalization, or visit counts; these domains therefore cannot enter a KCHS-developed transportable model. Part of the residual external gap is thus attributable to development-survey coverage rather than concept shift alone, and a development survey that captured multimorbidity and utilization could plausibly lift the transportable ceiling by ~0.01 AUROC.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:166:A survey-derived depression screener transports across country and pandemic with clinically useful discrimination and recoverable calibration. The cross-national discrimination ceiling (~0.77) is a property of the transportable predictor set—most consistent with shift in the predictor–outcome relationship, with residual contributions from differing measurement, development-survey coverage and complete-case selection—rather than a modeling failure. A low-burden 5- to 7-item version, paired with local recalibration, is a plausible scalable digital triage aid.
