docs/manuscript_17var.md:21:**Conclusions.** A Korea-developed depression screener transported to the US and across the pandemic with clinically useful discrimination and fully recoverable calibration. The cross-national ceiling (~0.77) persisted against sample size, algorithm choice, covariate-shift correction and an in-domain oracle, and is therefore a property of the transportable predictor set rather than a modeling failure. A low-burden 5- to 7-item version, paired with local recalibration, is a plausible scalable digital triage aid.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:50:### 2.3 Participants
docs/manuscript_17var.md:96:### 3.1 Participant characteristics
docs/manuscript_17var.md:97:Weighted depression prevalence (PHQ-9 ≥10): KCHS 3.6%, NHANES pre 8.4%, post 12.2% (Figure 1). The surveys differed markedly on measured covariates—e.g., mean BMI 23.7 (KCHS) vs 29.8 (NHANES), and weighted food-insecurity prevalence 3.7% in KCHS vs 16.4% and 17.2% in the NHANES cycles, computed among respondents with the item recorded (missing 22.1%, 6.0% and 6.3%, respectively; every characteristic in Table S6 uses available-case denominators)—consistent with genuinely distinct populations and, for food insecurity, with the single-item-vs-module measurement difference. DPQ item non-response was substantial in both NHANES cycles and much larger after the pandemic: among adults ≥20 years, 14.3% of the pre-pandemic and 32.7% of the post-pandemic respondents left three or more PHQ-9 items unanswered, yielding the complete-case analytic samples of 7,888 and 5,210 (item-level detail in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S10). Non-response was differential—higher among respondents with less than high-school education (20.6% pre, 47.3% post) and among Asian and Black respondents—so the external cohorts, and particularly the post-pandemic fairness estimates, should be read as complete-case results. Reassuringly, adding respondents missing only one or two items using their person-mean changed the observed prevalence by ≤0.1 percentage points in both cycles (unweighted: 9.23%→9.27% pre, 12.69%→12.78% post). Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S6.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:100:LightGBM was the primary model selected a priori by internal cross-validated AUROC (§2.8). Internal hold-out AUROC was **0.832 (95% CI 0.823–0.841)**, AUPRC 0.233, Brier 0.031, calibration slope 1.00, CITL +0.10 — **H1 met**.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:128:We localized the ceiling with an **in-domain oracle**: fitting NHANES on its own labels (a deliberate §2.10 violation, for diagnosis only) under cross-validation. Across logistic, penalized-logistic, histogram-gradient-boosting and LightGBM learners, the honest 5-fold cross-validated oracle reached only 0.765–0.772 (best learner 0.772, 95% CI 0.749–0.795 by the same rescaled cluster bootstrap). The KCHS-transferred model (pre 0.772, post 0.769) therefore already **matches the best in-domain performance any of these learners achieved when fitted directly to NHANES**. This is an empirical benchmark over the learners we tried, not a proof of a maximum; it is estimated on the pooled pre+post sample whereas the transported figures are cycle-specific, and its interval overlaps the transported estimates, so the comparison is approximate and cannot exclude a modest true gap. Matching sample size does not close the gap: reducing the Korean data to the NHANES sample size and cross-validating within it still yielded 0.823 at n=7,888 (the pre-pandemic cohort size) and 0.810 at n=13,098 (the pooled oracle size), rising to 0.830 only at n=200,000 (best of three learners, 5-fold weighted AUROC). At identical n, in other words, the same 17 predictors carry 0.81–0.82 of discrimination in Korea against an NHANES ceiling of ~0.77. Three further checks converged on the same conclusion: (i) covariate-shift correction by density-ratio reweighting of the KCHS training sample toward the NHANES covariate distribution (labels unused) *reduced* external AUROC (to 0.766/0.758), indicating the gap is not covariate shift; (ii) increasing the KCHS training set from 0.5M to 2M examples did not raise—and slightly lowered—external discrimination, consistent with source-specific overfitting rather than an estimation-limited ceiling; and (iii) adding the remaining harmonized-but-unused survey items did not help: public-assistance receipt alone left external AUROC unchanged (0.772/0.769), and adding all nine remaining candidates together slightly *reduced* it (−0.003 pre, −0.001 post in the exploration run). Deep tabular learners (a multilayer perceptron, TabNet, and an FT-Transformer) were additionally trained and transferred; all fell below gradient boosting internally and none exceeded it on both external cohorts (Multimedia Appendix 4), consistent with the model-agnostic ceiling. Sample size, model choice, covariate imbalance and omitted harmonized information were each tested and none accounts for the gap. The most consistent remaining explanation is **shift in the predictor–outcome relationship itself**—concept shift—although the surveys also differ in how several predictors are measured (§2.5) and the external cohorts are complete-case samples with differential depression-module non-response (§3.1), so measurement and selection effects cannot be separated from concept shift with these data. What the evidence does establish is that the gap is not closable by recalibration, which corrects calibration rather than rank ordering, nor by more data or a different learner.
