docs/manuscript_17var.md:19:**Results.** Gradient boosting (LightGBM) was the primary model. Internal AUROC was 0.832 (95% CI 0.823–0.841), meeting the ≥0.80 target (H1). External discrimination remained clinically useful but lower (pre 0.772, 95% CI 0.735–0.808; post 0.769, 0.739–0.799); calibration deteriorated (slope 0.66–0.68; calibration-in-the-large +0.49 and +1.11, ie, under-prediction) and was fully restored by intercept/slope recalibration (slope→0.99, CITL→0.00; H2, H3 supported). Against an independently tuned 14-predictor model, the three added predictors improved external discrimination (pre 0.749→0.772, Δ+0.023, 95% CI +0.013 to +0.033; post 0.753→0.769, Δ+0.016, +0.008 to +0.023; both *P*<.001). Applying the analysis plan's parsimony margin, with predictor order and item count fixed inside the development data, selected a 5-item model (0.809 internal, 0.742 pre, 0.746 post; H4 partially supported); a 7-item variant transports better (0.819/0.760/0.758) but was chosen post hoc. Leakage-controlled optimization (>450 experiments) did not exceed ~0.77 externally, and an oracle fitted directly to NHANES reached only 0.765–0.772, whereas the same predictors reached 0.81–0.82 in equally sized Korean samples.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:81:SHAP global importance [9] and cross-survey rank concordance of predictor importance (Spearman correlation between KCHS and each NHANES cycle) as a mechanistic view of transportability; fairness by weighted subgroup AUROC and calibration slope across sex, age, education, and (NHANES) race/ethnicity; and a parsimonious model for a low-burden self-administered form. Both the predictor order and the item count were determined entirely inside the development training set, which was split into a 450,000-record fitting set and a 50,000-record selection set: predictors were entered in descending order of global SHAP importance computed on a random 20,000-record subsample of the fitting set, refitted at every item count from 1 to 17, and the item count was chosen on selection-set AUROC. The item count follows the analysis plan's margin — the smallest set within 0.02 AUROC of the full model, subject to a 5–10 item ceiling; a variant using a stricter 0.01 margin is reported alongside it and flagged as post hoc (§3.6). The internal hold-out and both NHANES cohorts were used only for evaluation (§3.6). Positioned as a screening/triage aid, not a diagnostic.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:123:To avoid advantaging the larger predictor set through tuning, the 14-predictor comparator was given its own hyperparameter search under the identical internal protocol (§2.8; best internal CV-AUROC 0.824). The three added predictors improved external discrimination over this independently tuned 14-predictor model: pre 0.749→**0.772** (Δ+0.023, 95% CI +0.013 to +0.033; *P*<.001), post 0.753→**0.769** (Δ+0.016, 95% CI +0.008 to +0.023; *P*<.001); *P* values are from a paired rescaled cluster bootstrap with replicates centred on the null and a Monte-Carlo correction (2000 replicates), with internal AUROC rising 0.822→0.832. Independent tuning did not help the 14-predictor model—running it with the 17-predictor configuration gave nearly identical results (pre 0.750, post 0.754)—so the gain reflects predictor content rather than modeling strategy. Removing food insecurity alone (16-predictor model) retained most of the gain (pre 0.767, post 0.760), confirming the improvement does not depend on the single most weakly harmonized predictor.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:133:Applying the margin recommended in the analysis plan—the smallest set staying within 0.02 AUROC of the full model, subject to a 5–10 item ceiling—selects **five items**: self-rated health, age, sex, sleep duration and self-rated oral health. Evaluated afterwards on data that played no part in selection, this model achieved hold-out AUROC **0.809**, NHANES pre **0.742** and NHANES post **0.746**, against 0.832/0.773/0.769 for the full 17-predictor model fitted on the same data — a loss of 0.023 internally, 0.031 pre-pandemic and 0.023 post-pandemic. Because the protocol described the tolerable loss qualitatively rather than numerically, whether this satisfies **H4** is a judgment: the model meets the plan's selection margin internally, but the external loss is larger than the internal one, so we regard H4 as only partially supported.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:135:A stricter 0.01 margin would instead select seven items, adding employment and partnership, with hold-out 0.819 and external 0.760 (pre) and 0.758 (post) — losses of 0.013, 0.012 and 0.010. We report this variant because it transports better, but that comparison inspected the NHANES cohorts and is therefore post hoc: its external figures are exploratory rather than an unbiased estimate for a prospectively chosen seven-item form. The same caution applies to the observation that an eighth item, smoking, raises pre-pandemic discrimination to 0.770 while lowering post-pandemic discrimination to 0.753. Choosing between the five- and seven-item forms should be settled in new validation data. The full k=1–17 trajectory is tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S8.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:138:Discrimination was stable across specifications that alter modeling choices rather than the target condition (Figure 4): weighted training (0.831/0.773/0.764 internal/pre/post), missing-indicator imputation (0.833/0.770/0.767), single chained-equations imputation of the continuous predictors (0.833/0.770/0.767), and the 16- and 14-predictor variants (§3.4) all lay within 0.01 of the primary model internally. Restricting both training and evaluation to respondents aged ≥30 years *improved* discrimination (0.840/0.784/0.788), consistent with the lower subgroup performance observed at ages 20–39 (§3.8). Changing the outcome definition to PHQ-9 ≥5 lowered discrimination throughout (0.759/0.736/0.739) at a much higher weighted prevalence (16.1%/25.0%/32.1%), as expected when the target shifts from moderate-or-worse symptoms to any symptom burden; notably, the Korea–US gap narrows from about 0.06 AUROC at the ≥10 threshold to about 0.02 at ≥5 (0.759 vs 0.736–0.739), suggesting that the transportability loss is concentrated at the more severe end of the symptom distribution. Replacing current drinking with drinking at least twice weekly, with binge drinking, or entering all three indicators together — the alternative alcohol definitions listed in the analysis plan — changed discrimination by at most 0.003 externally (pre 0.769–0.772, post 0.769–0.770). In the pre-specified continuous-score analysis, the deployed classifier ranked the untransformed PHQ-9 total with weighted Spearman ρ=0.378 internally, 0.403 pre-pandemic and 0.439 post-pandemic, and a model trained directly on the continuous score performed similarly (ρ=0.402/0.403/0.424; weighted R² 0.216/0.166/0.102), so the transportability pattern is not an artifact of dichotomizing the outcome. Data-integrity fixes (§2.13) did not alter substantive conclusions. Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:143:Subgroup discrimination in KCHS was fairly uniform (weighted AUROC 0.785–0.859): sex (female 0.822, 95% CI 0.811–0.834; male 0.831, 0.814–0.847), education (0.813–0.830), and age (20–39 years 0.785, 0.764–0.807 < 40–59 years 0.828, 0.811–0.845 < ≥60 years 0.859, 0.847–0.871). In NHANES, discrimination was lower and far less precisely estimated. Pre-pandemic: Mexican American 0.697 (0.631–0.770; n=914), non-Hispanic Black 0.762 (0.729–0.804; n=2,096), other/multiracial 0.772 (0.679–0.868; n=381), other Hispanic 0.777 (0.736–0.817; n=813), non-Hispanic White 0.778 (0.731–0.822; n=2,798), non-Hispanic Asian 0.810 (0.719–0.874; n=886). Post-pandemic: Mexican American 0.689 (0.538–0.805; n=330), non-Hispanic Black 0.727 (0.646–0.788; n=598), non-Hispanic White 0.770 (0.723–0.811; n=3,215), other/multiracial 0.786 (0.701–0.863; n=340), other Hispanic 0.790 (0.751–0.845; n=499), non-Hispanic Asian 0.841 (0.651–0.988; n=228). Applying the fairness flag set in the analysis plan — a subgroup AUROC differing from the cohort value by more than 0.05 — only Mexican American respondents are flagged, in both cycles (−0.075 pre, −0.080 post). These intervals are wide and mutually overlapping, however, and every one of them overlaps the cohort-level interval, so the ordering should not be read as established differential performance; that the lowest point estimate falls in Mexican American respondents in both cycles nonetheless warrants monitoring as an equity caveat (Figure 6). Internal subgroup calibration was good (slope 0.97–1.02 across sex and age strata, 0.93–1.03 including education strata; subgroup CITL −0.02 to +0.26). In NHANES the subgroup calibration slopes were uniformly below 1 (0.55–0.78, one exception) and subgroup CITL was positive in every group except Mexican American pre-pandemic (up to +1.30), confirming that the external miscalibration is under-prediction of absolute risk and not only over-extreme ranking (§3.3). Exact subgroup values with CIs are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S9.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:145:On decision-curve analysis (weighted, with cohort-specific screen-all and screen-none default strategies), the model provided positive net benefit exceeding both defaults across a clinically relevant threshold-probability range in all three datasets (Figure 7). At the Youden-optimal threshold, computed over all observed scores rather than a coarse grid, sensitivity/specificity were 0.730/0.780 (internal), 0.733/0.687 (NHANES pre), and 0.739/0.667 (NHANES post); positive predictive value was low (0.110–0.236) and negative predictive value high (0.948–0.987), as expected for population screening at low prevalence (full operating characteristics across thresholds in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S1). Tuned to a triage-appropriate 85% sensitivity, the model flagged roughly half of the external population (pre 51%, post 55%) at a PPV of 0.14 (pre) and 0.19 (post)—a number needed to screen of 7.1 and 5.4—while the 5-item deployment model flagged somewhat more people for slightly lower yield (PPV 0.122 and 0.170; number needed to screen 8.2 and 5.9). After local intercept/slope recalibration, net benefit remained positive and exceeded both default strategies—screening everyone and screening no one—at threshold probabilities of 0.10–0.15 in every dataset; screen-all net benefit was in fact negative at these thresholds everywhere except NHANES post at 0.10, where the model still added +0.033 (Multimedia Appendix 3, Tables S4–S5). Clinical utility remains provisional pending prospective evaluation.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:163:The transportable predictor set is also bounded by the *development* survey's content. In a coverage analysis we extracted candidate domains available in NHANES but not harmonizable to KCHS—physician-diagnosed multimorbidity (asthma, arthritis, thyroid, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, liver disease, and cancer, excluding circulatory conditions) and healthcare utilization—and found that adding them raised the NHANES in-domain oracle from 0.765 to 0.778. KCHS, however, records only hypertension and diabetes among physician-diagnosed conditions (both already included) and does not collect a broader chronic-disease inventory, hospitalization, or visit counts; these domains therefore cannot enter a KCHS-developed transportable model. Part of the residual external gap is thus attributable to development-survey coverage rather than concept shift alone, and a development survey that captured multimorbidity and utilization could plausibly lift the transportable ceiling by ~0.01 AUROC.
